The try/catch handles synchronous throws. But `requestPointerLock()` in newer Chrome returns a Promise that can reject asynchronously. The wrapper returns the promise which, if rejected, is unhandled → pageerror. Fix: capture and catch the returned promise.

```js
requestLock() {
  if (this.isTouch) return;
  try {
    const p = this.canvas.requestPointerLock?.();
    if (p && typeof p.catch === 'function') p.catch(() => {});
  } catch { /* ignore */ }
}
```

